## CTO vs CODEX SPLIT

### CTO writes (you, Claude Code):
- All files in src/types/ (interfaces — the contract)
- src/services/TierRouter.ts
- src/services/ContextManager.ts
- src/services/MemoryExtractor.ts
- training/qualityValidator.ts
- training/regressionEval.ts
- adapters/AdapterRegistry.ts (Ed25519 verification logic)
- ALL unit and integration tests
- All PR reviews before any merge

### Codex generates (call via API or codex CLI):
- Boilerplate: package.json, tsconfig, vite config, eslint, prettier
- Component shells (no business logic)
- IndexedDB CRUD wrappers
- OllamaHealth utility
- PairBuilder (training data formatting)
- UpdateChannel client
- CSS/styles
- GitHub Actions CI yml

### Rule: Codex output is always treated as a PR. CTO reviews before merge. No exceptions.

---

## GUARDRAIL HIERARCHY (non-negotiable)

1. Pre-commit: ESLint + Prettier (Husky blocks bad commits)
2. All Codex output reviewed by CTO before merge
3. CI: lint → typecheck → test → build (all must pass)
4. Training: pair quality check → min 50 pairs → train
5. Post-train: MMLU eval → accept/reject (max 2% regression)
6. Beta: 5 conversation smoke test before prod swap
7. Production: CEO manual sign-off required
8. Live: automatic rollback on regression

If any guardrail fails, the chain stops. No exceptions.

---

## GIT WORKFLOW (strict)

1. CTO implements changes locally in ~/my-ai/airia
2. Run full test suite: pnpm test (all packages)
3. Present diff to CEO for review
4. CEO approves explicitly
5. Only then: git push origin main

NEVER push without CEO approval.
NEVER self-merge.
NEVER skip tests before presenting to CEO.

---

## KEY ARCHITECTURE DECISIONS (locked)

- Local-first: all inference and storage on-device by default
- TierRouter: detects local/cloud/free at startup, routes all service calls
- KVStorage abstraction: TierRouter uses injected storage (InMemoryStorage for tests, LocalStorage for browser)
- DPO over PPO: preference pairs, no separate reward model (VRAM budget)
- Unsloth for QLoRA: 2x faster, ~40% less VRAM than vanilla HF
- IndexedDB via idb: PWA-native, offline-first
- Ed25519 for adapter signing: separate key from update channel key
- Memory layer: personal facts/preferences extracted post-response, injected into system prompt
- Skill gates: literal milestones (conversation count, feedback signals, fine-tune cycles)
- "Buddy not tool": all copy and UX decisions reflect this — AIrIA is a companion, not a second brain
- Free tier adaptation: context-layer memory (immediate) + skill adapter marketplace (LoRA adapters we sign and publish)

